@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE PINNED JOB CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* Hatch keypad: brought up by knocking on the specific locked hatch a crack job sent you to. Key in
       the code the Fixer quoted; right code pops the hatch (job → PickedUp), wrong code buzzes red. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="pin-backdrop" @onclick="CancelPin">
            @* #997 wave 9 · THE KEYPAD'S Cancel IS THE SHELL'S DISMISS. The plainest Close left in the client:
               one way out, last direct child of the pad, wired to the same CancelPin the backdrop behind it
               already calls. Wave 4's three parameters and nothing else — `Bare` (the pad's own
               `.pin-pad-hatch` IS its head and the shell must not put a second one above it),
               `StopClicks="true"` (the stopPropagation that was typed on this div), and the Close.

               NOT ONE WORD MOVED: "Cancel" and its title are the exact strings the hand-typed button carried.
               And it keeps its own height — `.pin-pad-cancel` is not a `.btn`, so the shell's chrome rule
               gives it `line-height: 1`, which is the same number its own `font: 600 .85rem/1 monospace` was
               already setting. Measured in the browser and written up in the PR, not assumed. *@
            <OverlayShell class="pin-pad" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close" OnClose="CancelPin"
                          DismissClass="pin-pad-cancel" DismissFace="Cancel"
                          DismissTitle="Back off — leave the hatch locked">
                <div class="pin-pad-hatch">@(_pinHatch?.Label ?? "LOCKED HATCH")</div>
                <div class="pin-pad-sub">ENTER ACCESS CODE</div>
                <div class="pin-pad-display">@PinDisplay</div>
                <div class="pin-pad-keys">
                    @foreach (string d in new[] { "1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7", "8", "9" })
                    {
                        string digit = d;
                        <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key" title="Key this digit into the access code" @onclick="() => PinPush(digit)">@digit</button>
                    }
                    <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key pin-pad-key-fn" title="Clear the code and start over" @onclick="PinClear">C</button>
                    <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key" title="Key a zero into the access code" @onclick='() => PinPush("0")'>0</button>
                    <button type="button" class="pin-pad-key pin-pad-key-go" title="Try this code on the hatch" @onclick="SubmitPin">↵</button>
                </div>
                @* #736 · The buzz, said on the pad. A wrong code leaves the pad up and blanks the four slots,
                   and the sentence that explained why was pulsing to the HUD under this backdrop. *@
                @if (_pinOutcome is { } pinSaid)
                {
                    <div class="panel-outcome">@pinSaid</div>
                }
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    // ── WHAT THESE PARAMETERS ARE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // #251 item 1 · Every [Parameter] below carries a member of the page under THE MEMBER'S OWN NAME —
    // a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate with its own signature, a variable the
    // page's `@if` binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it. That is the whole trick of this
    // refactor: it is what let the markup above move out of Map.razor without a single character of it
    // changing, and what lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as they read it
    // when it lived in the page. A parameter that needs saying more than that says it on its own line.

    [Parameter] public DeckPlan.ConsoleSpot? _pinHatch { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string? _pinOutcome { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Action CancelPin { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action PinClear { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public string PinDisplay { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action<string> PinPush { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action SubmitPin { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
